Done, Sir — I created a safe, opt-in STT+TTS package inside jason_programs so nothing in prototype-jason-ui was changed. You can review, copy, or ask me to apply the changes into your UI when you're ready.

What I created
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/README.txt — overview, install options, safety notes.
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/stt_tts_bridge.js — main module (init-only; will not auto-start mic unless you ask).
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/demo.html — small standalone demo page you can open locally to test quickly.
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/styles.css — minimal mic button styles.

Why I kept it separate
- You asked not to risk breaking the current system. The module is separate and opt-in: you must add the script tag and call init() from your prototype to enable STT/TTS.
- I included comments and a README so you can see exactly what to paste where.

Quick summary of features implemented
- Both STT and TTS using the browser Web Speech APIs (client-side, no keys).
- STT configured for continuous listen-until-stop (recognition.continuous = true, interim results enabled).
- Interim transcripts are placed into #chatInput so you see live transcription.
- Module dispatches a custom event 'jason_stt_final' with final text when recognition produces final transcript or on recognition end.
- TTS selects a likely en-US voice and heuristically prefers male-sounding voices; tuned pitch/rate for less-robotic sound (adjustable).
- TTS auto-plays assistant responses when you call the speak() API (option autoPlayTTS true).
- Mic button can be attached automatically (minimal, non-invasive styling) or you can use your own button.
- The module logs helpful diagnostic messages to console rather than throwing UI errors.

Files I added (paths)
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/README.txt
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/stt_tts_bridge.js
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/demo.html
- jason_programs/STT+TTS/styles.css

How to test now (quick)
1. Open jason_programs/STT+TTS/demo.html in Chrome/Edge (recommended). It demonstrates start/stop and TTS speak.
2. Open DevTools console for logs. The demo uses en-US and tries to select a male voice.

Exact, safe steps to integrate into prototype-jason-ui (copy/paste)
I’ll give two small, safe edits you can make yourself. I included precise code and comments so you know where to paste.

A) Add the module script tag (index.html)
Place this line just before the existing
    <script src="script.js"></script>
in prototype-jason-ui/index.html (so the module is loaded before your main script):

    <!-- STT+TTS bridge (optional) -->
    <script src="../jason_programs/STT+TTS/stt_tts_bridge.js"></script>

Note: the relative path above assumes prototype-jason-ui and jason_programs are sibling folders (based on the project layout). If your layout differs, adjust the path accordingly.

B) Initialize the module and wire TTS/STT into your UI (script.js)
Open prototype-jason-ui/script.js and locate the DOMContentLoaded handler (there is one at the top). Near the end of that handler — after functions are defined and before loadConversationHistory() and initThreeGlobe() are called — add the following snippet. I mark it clearly with comments so it’s easy to find and remove if needed.

Paste this inside the existing document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => { ... }); block, for example near the bottom, before loadConversationHistory();:

// --- STT+TTS integration (paste start) ---
// Optional: initialize STT+TTS bridge (module is opt-in and safe).
// If you want it disabled, leave commented or don't include the script tag in index.html.
let jasonSttTts = null;
if (window.JasonSTTTS) {
  try {
    jasonSttTts = window.JasonSTTTS.init({
      chatInputSelector: '#chatInput',
      chatMessagesSelector: '#chatMessages',
      language: 'en-US',
      continuous: true,
      interimResults: true,
      autoSendOnStop: false,   // set true to auto-send final transcript
      autoPlayTTS: true,       // auto-play assistant responses
      preferMale: true,
      attachMicButton: true,   // set false if you prefer your own button
      startOnInit: false       // safe default: do not request mic permission automatically
    });

    // Example: speak assistant reply automatically.
    // Add this line inside your existing fetch response success block where you appendMessage('assistant', data.reply);
    // For your file: after appendMessage(\"assistant\", data.reply); add:
    //   if (jasonSttTts) jasonSttTts.speak(data.reply);
    //
    // The exact location in your script.js is inside handleSendMessage(), after appendMessage(\"assistant\", data.reply).
    //
    // Also optional: if you want to auto-send speech result when recognition ends, listen for the custom event:
    window.addEventListener('jason_stt_final', (e) => {
      // e.detail.text contains the final transcript string.
      // Place it into the chat input (non-destructive) and optionally send.
      try {
        const text = e.detail.text || '';
        if (!text) return;
        const chatInput = document.querySelector('#chatInput');
        if (chatInput) {
          chatInput.value = text;
          chatInput.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
        }
        // If you prefer automatic send, call the same function your UI uses:
        // handleSendMessage(); // <-- uncomment this line to auto-send final transcript
      } catch (err) {
        console.warn('jason_stt_final handler failed', err);
      }
    });

  } catch (e) {
    console.warn('STT+TTS init failed', e);
  }
}
// --- STT+TTS integration (paste end) ---

Important places I recommend editing in your existing code (safe, commented examples)
- Where to call TTS for assistant replies:
  In handleSendMessage() after the line
    appendMessage("assistant", data.reply);
  add:
    if (jasonSttTts) jasonSttTts.speak(data.reply);

- If you want STT to automatically send recognized speech when final, uncomment handleSendMessage() inside the jason_stt_final listener above. I left it commented to avoid unexpected sends.

Notes, limitations, and browser compatibility
- Browsers: Chrome / Edge have the best support for SpeechRecognition + SpeechSynthesis. Firefox supports TTS but not the SpeechRecognition API (desktop Firefox lacks webkitSpeechRecognition). On iOS Safari, recognition support is limited.
- Continuous recognition can still stop in some browsers due to inactivity or browser policy. Module does not force auto-restart — that behavior risks looping permission prompts. I can optionally add an auto-restart with safe backoff if you prefer.
- Voice availability varies by OS/browser. The module picks a likely en-US male-sounding voice if available. You can adjust pitch and rate in stt_tts_bridge.js (speak() defaults: pitch 0.95, rate 0.96).
- Microphone permission: the browser will prompt. The module does not request permission on page load unless you set startOnInit: true. Use the mic button to request permission manually.

What I suggest next (pick one)
- You copy the files into prototype-jason-ui using the instructions above and test with demo.html — I gave exact snippets and comments.
- Or I can apply the small, non-invasive edits directly into prototype-jason-ui:
  - Add the script include into index.html and
  - Insert the init + event hookup snippet in script.js (and add the one-line TTS call inside handleSendMessage).
  Tell me to proceed and I will make the exact edits and then re-open the changed files for you to review. I will not start mic or run anything beyond writing files.

If you want me to apply the edits now, say "Apply" and confirm:
- Add script tag to prototype-jason-ui/index.html? (yes/no)
- Insert init snippet into prototype-jason-ui/script.js and add one-line TTS call into handleSendMessage? (yes/no)

I kept everything commented and documented so you can copy with low risk. If you want any parameter changes (different pitch/rate, autoSend true, or prefer female voice), tell me and I’ll update the module before you integrate it.

