- Apply the small integration change into prototype-jason-ui (add Enable Audio and make TTS robust) — I will only make the edits if you say “Apply”.

Tell me:
- Which browser and OS you tested with (Chrome/Edge/Firefox/Safari + Windows/Mac/Android/iOS)?
- The console outputs for the voice-list check and any [JasonSTTTS] logs, or say “Apply” if you want me to add the fallback + Enable Audio button now.

Ready when you are, Sir.

Yes, Sir — I read the files. Short answer: you called the initializer, but two small things prevent TTS from playing inside prototype-jason-ui.

What I checked
- prototype-jason-ui/index.html includes stt_tts_bridge.js (at the bottom) and includes script.js (earlier). I opened both files.
- prototype-jason-ui/script.js contains a DOMContentLoaded handler that calls window.JasonSTTTS.init(...) — so you did call the initializer after DOMContentLoaded as the README suggested.

Why TTS still doesn't play
1) You call init(...) but you do not store the returned module API. The bridge returns an API object with speak(...). Your script calls init but never keeps the returned object, so later code cannot call speak(text).
2) Your UI code that receives the assistant reply (appendMessage("assistant", data.reply)) does not call the module's speak(...) method. The bridge does not automatically speak every appendMessage; it exposes speak() which must be invoked when an assistant response arrives.

Small additional note about script order
- Right now stt_tts_bridge.js is included after script.js in index.html. That still usually works because both scripts finish loading before DOMContentLoaded fires, but placing stt_tts_bridge.js before script.js is the safer order recommended in the README (so init is guaranteed available as soon as script.js defines its DOMContentLoaded handler). It's optional in your current setup, but I recommend moving the bridge <script> above script.js to match the README.

Exactly what to change (copy/paste)
1) Capture the API instance in script.js:
Replace the existing init call block
    if (window.JasonSTTTS) {
      window.JasonSTTTS.init({
        chatInputSelector: '#chatInput',
        chatMessagesSelector: '#chatMessages',
        language: 'en-US',
        continuous: true,
        autoSendOnStop: false,
        autoPlayTTS: true,
        preferMale: true,
        attachMicButton: true
      });
    }

With this (store instance globally):
    if (window.JasonSTTTS) {
      // keep the instance so we can call speak(...) later
      window.jasonSttTts = window.JasonSTTTS.init({
        chatInputSelector: '#chatInput',
        chatMessagesSelector: '#chatMessages',
        language: 'en-US',
        continuous: true,
        autoSendOnStop: false,
        autoPlayTTS: true,
        preferMale: true,
        attachMicButton: true
      });
    }

2) Call speak when assistant reply arrives
Find the place where you append assistant replies (inside handleSendMessage after the response):
    appendMessage("assistant", data.reply);

Right after that line add:
    // Auto-play assistant voice if STT+TTS was initialized
    if (window.jasonSttTts && typeof window.jasonSttTts.speak === 'function') {
      try { window.jasonSttTts.speak(String(data.reply || '')); } catch (e) { console.warn('TTS speak failed', e); }
    }

Notes about autoplay / voices
- If you already tested demo.html and it plays TTS, voices are available and autoplay likely allowed in your environment. If TTS still doesn't play after these changes, check the DevTools console for [JasonSTTTS] logs and the speechSynthesis voice list (I can add a quick debug button if you'd like).
- If you prefer, set autoPlayTTS: true (you already did) and keep startOnInit behavior as you want; the important part is that speak(...) needs to be invoked.

